{
  "name": "Nebula",
  "notes": "Two things that did not work until recently, in one patch. FIRST, a scale-26 noise field under a zoom that breathes down to 0.18 -- an effective scale of about 144, seven times past what 90 LEDs can resolve. `PerlinEmitter` used to point-sample regardless of the footprint a warp handed it, so this looked like per-pixel hash the moment the zoom went low; it now fades unresolvable detail toward the mean instead, so the field goes SOFT as it magnifies rather than going to noise. SECOND, `colour.wrap` on, which is what makes the palette a ring: seven entries and NO repeat of the first at the end, because with wrap on the last entry's neighbour is the first and repeating it would give a flat spot a seventh of the wheel wide. The offset cycles endlessly on a ramp at depth 0.5 -- and that is exact rather than lucky, since `paletteOffset` is a Wrap parameter and a sawtooth from -0.5 to +0.5 wraps to the same value at both ends, so there is no jump where the ramp resets. L1 is a coarse field screened over the top for depth.",
  "layers": {
    "0": {
      "emitter": {
        "type": "perlin",
        "params": {
          "scale": 26,
          "time": 0,
          "offset": 0
        }
      },
      "colour": {
        "index": "u",
        "wrap": true,
        "params": {
          "paletteSpan": 0.2,
          "paletteOffset": 0.5
        },
        "palette": {
          "entries": [
            "4500ff",
            "7a00ff",
            "ff00c6",
            "ff3c00",
            "ff9900",
            "00f1ff",
            "0039ff"
          ]
        }
      },
      "warps": [
        {
          "type": "scale",
          "params": {
            "zoom": 0.15,
            "centre": 0.5
          }
        }
      ],
      "blend": "add",
      "opacity": 1,
      "order": 0
    },
    "1": {
      "emitter": {
        "type": "perlin",
        "params": {
          "scale": 4,
          "time": 0,
          "offset": 0
        }
      },
      "colour": {
